.\" OpenBSD-style concise man page
.Dd $Mdocdate$
.Dt READLINK 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm readlink
.Nd print value of a symbolic link or canonical file name
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm readlink
.Op Fl fenm
.Op Fl nqvz
.Ar file ...
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
utility prints the target of a symbolic link.
When used with the canonicalize options, it prints the resolved
absolute pathname.
.Pp
If
.Nm
is invoked without a canonicalize option, the given
.Ar file
must be a symbolic link.
With a canonicalize option,
.Ar file
need not be a symbolic link.
.Pp
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl f , Fl Fl canonicalize
Canonicalize by following every symlink in every component of the
given name recursively.
All but the last component must exist.
.It Fl e , Fl Fl canonicalize-existing
Canonicalize by following every symlink in every component of the
given name recursively.
All components must exist.
.It Fl m , Fl Fl canonicalize-missing
Canonicalize by following every symlink in every component of the
given name recursively, without requirements on component existence.
.It Fl n , Fl Fl no-newline
Do not output the trailing newline.
.It Fl q , Fl Fl quiet , Fl Fl silent
Suppress most error messages.
.It Fl v , Fl Fl verbose
Report error messages.
.It Fl z , Fl Fl zero
End each output line with NUL, not newline.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std readlink
.Sh EXAMPLES
Print the target of a symbolic link:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ readlink /usr/bin/vi
/usr/bin/nvi
.Ed
.Pp
Resolve the canonical path of a symlink:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ readlink -f /usr/bin/vi
/usr/bin/nvi
.Ed
.Pp
Resolve with NUL-terminated output for use with xargs:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ readlink -fz /usr/bin/vi | xargs -0 echo
/usr/bin/nvi
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr ln 1 ,
.Xr realpath 1 ,
.Xr stat 1
.Sh STANDARDS
The
.Nm
utility is not specified by POSIX.
It is compatible with the GNU coreutils
.Nm
command.
.Sh AUTHORS
.An "vibeutils implementation by Travis Cole"
